"Checking your browser - reCAPTCHA (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2196422/)\nciteturn40view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2196422/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 3\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://rnajc.ucsf.edu/sites/rnajc.ucsf.edu/files/nbt.3973_0.pdf)\nciteturn40view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 7; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn36view3\",\"pattern\":\"ICOS\"}); Total lines: 942\nL220@P1: BRIEF COMMUNICATIONS\nL221@P1-2: © 2017 Nature America, Inc., part of Springer Nature. All rights reserved.938 VOLUME 35 NUMBER 10 OCTOBER 2017 NATURE BIOTECHNOLOGY\nL222@P2: aCD27 and TCR stimulation. Naive CD8+ T cells from the blood of\nL225@P2: (Supplementary Table 1). After sorting for live cells by flow cytom\u0002etry, we loaded the cells onto the 10x Genomics Chromium Controller\nL226@P2: instrument.\nL227@P2: We used REAP-seq to analyze cells treated with aCD27\nL228@P2: (Supplementary Table 4). For each donor, the aCD27-treated and\nL229@P2: untreated samples were merged into a digital gene or protein expression\nL230@P2: matrix and unsupervised clustering was performed (Supplementary\nL231@P2: Fig. 10b). Cells were color-coded by the different treatment conditions\nL232@P2: to visualize co-localization of cells within the t-SNE plots (Fig. 2a and\nL233@P2: Supplementary Fig. 11). Although the number of protein markers was\nL234@P2: far fewer than the number of genes measured, there was clear visual\nL235@P2: separation between cells from the different treatment conditions when\nL236@P2: only protein expression data were used for clustering. When mRNA was\nL237@P2: clustered using the smaller set of protein markers there was no visual\nL238@P2: separation between aCD27-treated and untreated cells in donors 2 and\nL239@P2: 3 (Supplementary Fig. 11b).\nL240@P2: Genes and proteins differentially expressed in the aCD27-treated\nL241@P2: and untreated naive CD8+ T cells for each donor were identified\nL242@P2: 78\nL243@P2: 3 37\nL244@P2: 56\nL245@P2: 29\nL246@P2: 5 3\nL247@P2: Donor 3\nL248@P2: Donor 2 Donor 1\nL249@P2: DE genes\nL250@P2: outlier cluster\nL251@P2: c\nL252@P2: a\nL253@P2: DE proteins\nL254@P2: outlier cluster\nL255@P2: Donor 2\nL256@P2: Donor 1 Donor 3\nL257@P2: CD27**\nL258@P2: CD5\nL259@P2: CD8\nL260@P2: ICOS\nL261@P2: CD7\nL262@P2: CD2**\nL263@P2: HLA-DR**\nL264@P2: CD33\nL265@P2: CD34\nL266@P2: CD123\nL267@P2: CD117\nL268@P2: CD13\nL269@P2: CD38\nL270@P2: CD155\nL271@P2: CD40\nL272@P2: CD57\nL273@P2: PDL1\nL274@P2: Donor 1\nL275@P2: Donor 2\nL276@P2: Donor 3\nL277@P2: e\nL278@P2: f\nL279@P2: d\nL280@P2: 411922\nL281@P2: Decreased Increased\nL282@P2: 1.5\nL283@P2: 1\nL284@P2: 0.5\nL285@P2: 0\nL286@P2: -0.5\nL287@P2: -1\nL288@P2: -1.5\nL289@P2: Rest of\nL290@P2: Cells\nL291@P2: Outlier Cluster\nL292@P2: Donor 3\nL293@P2: CD27\nL294@P2: IL32\nL295@P2: CD52\nL296@P2: CORO1A\nL297@P2: TMSB4X\nL298@P2: CD3D\nL299@P2: LCK\nL300@P2: CD2\nL301@P2: FCER1A\nL302@P2: CLU\nL303@P2: GATA2\nL304@P2: ITGA2B\nL305@P2: FHL2\nL306@P2: SH3BGRL\nL307@P2: CSF2RB\nL308@P2: NFE2\nL309@P2: MINPP1\nL310@P2: NCF4\nL311@P2: PKIG\nL312@P2: PDLIM1\nL313@P2: H1F0\nL314@P2: BLVRB\nL315@P2: HBD\nL316@P2: GATA1\nL317@P2: MPP1\nL318@P2: SLC40A1\nL319@P2: TPSAB1\nL320@P2: AKR1C3\nL321@P2: GLUL\nL322@P2: HLA−B\nL323@P2: PRSS57\nL324@P2: APOC1\nL325@P2: HLA−DQA1\nL326@P2: TSC22D1\nL327@P2: HLA−DPA1\nL328@P2: HLA−DRA\nL329@P2: CD74\nL330@P2: SNCA\nL331@P2: HLA−DPB1\nL332@P2: EPSTI1\nL333@P2: B2M\nL334@P2: HLA−DQB1\nL335@P2: LGALS9\nL336@P2: LAPTM4A\nL337@P2: IL1B\nL338@P2: IFITM3\nL339@P2: GFI1B\nL340@P2: TIMP1\nL341@P2: IFIT3\nL342@P2: HLA−DRB1\nL343@P2: HLA−DMA\nL344@P2: UBE2L6\nL345@P2: NMI\nL346@P2: SOCS2\nL347@P2: CD63\nL348@P2: TAP1\nL349@P2: Donor 2\nL350@P2: Donor 1\nL351@P2: Donor 2\nL352@P2: Donor 1\nL353@P2: Donor 3\nL354@P2: 101\nL355@P2: 39\nL356@P2: 251\nL357@P2: 74 59\nL358@P2: 145\nL359@P2: 207\nL360@P2: Donor 2 Donor 1\nL361@P2: Donor 3\nL362@P2: LGALS1\nL363@P2: CCL22\nL364@P2: TK1\nL365@P2: MALAT1\nL366@P2: DDX5\nL367@P2: CCL4\nL368@P2: CCL5\nL369@P2: IL7R**\nL370@P2: MKi67\nL371@P2: AIF1\nL372@P2: EIF4A2\nL373@P2: CDKN3\nL374@P2: LST1\nL375@P2: SYNE2\nL376@P2: DE genes\nL377@P2: 3 4 2 3\nL378@P2: Donor 1\nL379@P2: Donor 3\nL380@P2: Donor 2 CD25\nL381@P2: ICOS\nL382@P2: CD28\nL383@P2: CD45RO\nL384@P2: CD70\nL385@P2: CD155\nL386@P2: CD4\nL387@P2: GITR\nL388@P2: PD1\nL389@P2: CD3\nL390@P2: CD5\nL391@P2: CD27\nL392@P2: CD45RA\nL393@P2: IL7R**\nL394@P2: CD73\nL395@P2: CD69\nL396@P2: DE proteins\nL397@P2: b\nL398@P2: Donor 1 Donor 2 Donor 3\nL399@P2: Treated aCD27\nL400@P2: Untreated aCD27\nL401@P2: Protein expression level\nL402@P2: −20\nL403@P2: 0\nL404@P2: 20\nL405@P2: 40\nL406@P2: −20 0 20 40\nL407@P2: −20\nL408@P2: 0\nL409@P2: 20\nL410@P2: 40\nL411@P2: −25 0 25\nL412@P2: −20 0 20 40\nL413@P2: −50\nL414@P2: -25\nL415@P2: 0\nL416@P2: 25\nL417@P2: ICOS mRNA\nL418@P2: 0 2 4 6 0 2 4 6 0 2 4 6\nL419@P2: 0 2 4 6 0 2 4 6 0 2 4 6\nL420@P2: 0 2 4 6 0 2 4 6 0 2 4 6\nL421@P2: CD27 CD45RA CD45RO\nL422@P2: 0 2 4 6 0 2 4 6 0 2 4 6\nL423@P2: -4 -2 0 2\nL424@P2: Protein\nL425@P2: 0\nL426@P2: ICOS\nL427@P2: 96.6%\nL428@P2: 97.4%\nL429@P2: 81.1%\nL430@P2: 92.4%\nL431@P2: 58.6%\nL432@P2: 77.8%\nL433@P2: 18.3%\nL434@P2: 5.8%\nL435@P2: 18.8%\nL436@P2: 13.7%\nL437@P2: 8.3%\nL438@P2: 8.1%\nL439@P2: **\nL440@P2: **\nL441@P2: **\nL442@P2: Donor 1 Donor 2 Donor 3\nL443@P2: Donor 1 Donor 2 Donor 3\nL444@P2: h i\nL445@P2: Treated aCD27\nL446@P2: Untreated aCD27\nL447@P2: Outlier\nL448@P2: cluster\nL449@P2: Outlier\nL450@P2: cluster\nL451@P2: Outlier\nL452@P2: cluster\nL453@P2: STAT1\nL454@P2: IRF1\nL455@P2: ISG20\nL456@P2: LTB\nL457@P2: HLA-B\nL458@P2: HLA-E\nL459@P2: B2M\nL460@P2: MIR155HG\nL461@P2: t-SNE1 Untreated Treated\nL462@P2: t-SNE2\nL463@P2: 17\nL464@P2: .\nL465@P2: .\nL466@P2: .\nL467@P2: g\nL468@P2: t-SNE1\nL469@P2: t-SNE2\nL470@P2: High\nL471@P2: Low\nL472@P2: HLA-DR CD34 CD123 CD117 CD33\nL473@P2: Outlier\nL474@P2: cluster\nL475@P2: Donor 1 Donor 3\nL476@P2: Outlier cluster\nL477@P2: Donor 2\nL478@P2: Outlier\nL479@P2: cluster\nL480@P2: Figure 2 REAP-seq characterization of ex vivo-activated naive CD8+ T cells with aCD27. (a) t-SNE visualization plots based on protein expression for\nL481@P2: each of the three donors. Blue dots indicate cells treated with aCD27 (donor 1: 4,246; donor 2: 4,044; donor 3: 3,550 cells) and magenta indicates cells\nL482@P2: not treated with aCD27 (donor 1: 950; donor 2: 622; donor 3: 406 cells). (b,c) Venn diagram showing the number of differentially expressed (DE) genes\nL483@P2: (n = 74) or proteins (n = 16, 17 AbBs, where two were different CD4 AbBs) shared across the three individual donors. Differentially expressed genes and\nL484@P2: proteins had adjusted P-values < 0.01 (corrected for multiple testing using the Bonferroni correction) and fold changes greater than 1.3 (threshold used for\nL485@P2: differential expression). (d) Violin plots showing expression distribution of CD27, CD45RO, CD45RA, and ICOS for untreated or treated aCD27 cells for each\nL486@P2: donor. Each dot represents an individual cell. Expression levels are log transformed, first scaling each cell to a total of 1 × 104 molecules. (e) Histograms\nL487@P2: showing ICOS protein and gene expression distribution in aCD27-treated (blue) and untreated (orange) cells. On the histogram the red dotted line shows\nL488@P2: the separation between signal and background noise and cells to the right of the line express ICOS. (f) Venn diagram showing the overlap of 17 differentially\nL489@P2: expressed proteins (19 AbB where three were different CD8 AbBs) in the outlier cluster compared to all other cells across all three donors (fold change > 1.5\nL490@P2: and adjusted P < 0.01, corrected for multiple testing using the Bonferroni correction). (g) Expression of five selected upregulated proteins (HLA-DR, CD34,\nL491@P2: CD123, CD117, CD33) in the outlier cluster was projected on the t-SNE visualization plots from a. The outlier cluster consisted of 116 cells in donor 1\nL492@P2: (2.2% total cells), 78 cells in donor 2 (1.7% of total cells), and 211 cells in donor 3 (5.3% total cells) (h) Venn diagram showing the overlap of differentially\nL493@P2: expressed genes in the outlier cluster compared to all other cells for each donor (fold ratio > 1.5, adjusted P-value < 0.01 corrected for multiple testing using\nL494@P2: the Bonferroni correction). (i) Heatmap showing the average scaled expression across all cells in the outlier cluster versus the rest of the cells for the 56\nL495@P2: differentially expressed genes. Red indicates genes upregulated, blue indicates genes downregulated. ** indicates markers differentially expressed at both\nL496@P2: protein and mRNA level.\nL497@P2: BRIEF COMMUNICATIONS\nL498@P2-3: © 2017 Nature America, Inc., part of Springer Nature. All rights reserved.NATURE BIOTECHNOLOGY VOLUME 35 NUMBER 10 OCTOBER 2017 939\nL499@P3: as those having adjusted P-values < 0.01 and fold changes > 1.3\nL500@P3: (Supplementary Fig. 12 and Supplementary Discussion). Among\nL501@P3: all three donors, there were 74 overlapping differentially expressed\nL502@P3: genes (Fig. 2b and Supplementary Fig. 13). In this overlapping gene\nL503@P3: set, the cell proliferation marker MKI67 was upregulated in aCD27-\nL504@P3: treated cells, agreeing with previous findings where T cells co-acti\u0002vated by TCR and CD27 were shown to induce a remarkable level\nL505@P3: of proliferation16. This unbiased scRNA-seq approach enabled us to\nL506@P3: see differential expression in unexpected genes such as MALAT1,\nL507@P3: SYNE2, LST1, and TK1 which would not have been included in a pre\u0002specified antibody panel. Also, MIR155HG, a non-coding RNA that\nL508@P3: cannot be detected at the protein level, showed decreased expression\nL509@P3: levels in cells treated with aCD27.\nL510@P3: REAP-seq protein analysis identified 16 differentially expressed pro\u0002teins overlapping across the three donors (Fig. 2c and Supplementary\nL511@P3: Figs. 14 and 15a) when cells were treated with aCD27. Interleukin\nL512@P3: (IL)-7R was consistently downregulated at both the protein and\nL513@P3: mRNA level (Supplementary Figs. 16, 17 and 18a) and there was a\nL514@P3: decrease in CD27 expression, which was confirmed by flow cytometry\nL515@P3: (Supplementary Fig. 19). We observed the loss of naive T-cell marker\nL516@P3: CD45RA expression and gain-of-effector memory T-cell marker\nL517@P3: CD45RO expression (Fig. 2d). Also, we found that a substantial num\u0002ber of CD8+ T cells treated with aCD27 cells expressed CD4 and CD25,\nL518@P3: a finding we confirmed by flow cytometry (Supplementary Figs. 14b,\nL519@P3: 18b, and 20).\nL520@P3: The levels of mRNA and protein did not always correlate\nL521@P3: (Supplementary Figs. 16 and 21), and protein quantification was\nL522@P3: more sensitive for markers with lower-abundance mRNA transcripts\nL523@P3: such as ICOS. ICOS is an immune checkpoint protein that has been\nL524@P3: shown to increase in cell surface expression upon costimulation with\nL525@P3: aCD27 and T-cell receptor (TCR) signaling16. We found an increase in\nL526@P3: ICOS expression at the protein level but not at the transcriptional level\nL527@P3: (Fig. 2e). Increased sensitivity in protein measurements may be due to\nL528@P3: longer protein half-lives and higher absolute protein abundance com\u0002pared to mRNA9. In addition, the protein assay may benefit from signal\nL529@P3: amplification owing to more than one DNA barcode conjugated to each\nL530@P3: antibody (an average of three DNA barcodes per antibody).\nL531@P3: To demonstrate how REAP-seq can be used to characterize unknown\nL532@P3: cellular populations, we used REAP-seq to identify a small population\nL533@P3: of cells present with our enriched naive CD8+ T cells (outlier clusters\nL534@P3: circled in Fig. 2a). In all three donors there were 17 shared differen\u0002tially expressed proteins between this outlier cluster and the rest of the\nL535@P3: cells (Fig. 2f), with a pattern suggestive of common myeloid progenitors\nL536@P3: (CMPs)17 (Fig. 2g), having high relative expression of CD34, CD38,\nL537@P3: CD123, CD117, CD13, CD33, and HLA-DR.\nL538@P3: At the mRNA level there were 56 overlapping genes that were dif\u0002ferentially expressed in the outlier cluster in all three donors (Fig. 2h,i),\nL539@P3: and three of these (HLA-DRA, CD27, and CD2) were also differentially\nL540@P3: expressed at the protein level (Supplementary Fig. 22). Together, the\nL541@P3: differentially expressed genes and proteins showed enrichment in the\nL542@P3: transcriptional regulation of megakaryopoiesis (false-discovery rate,\nL543@P3: FDR = 1.2 × 10–5, MetaCore) (Supplementary Fig. 23), the process\nL544@P3: by which mature megakaryocytes (MKs) develop from the common\nL545@P3: myeloid progenitor.\nL546@P3: An approach similar to REAP-seq was recently published that uses\nL547@P3: DNA-barcoded antibodies together with high-throughput scRNA\u0002seq18. A difference between the methods is how the DNA barcode is\nL548@P3: conjugated to the antibody. REAP-seq minimizes steric hindrance and\nL549@P3: potential crosstalk by using unidirectional chemistry that creates a small,\nL550@P3: stable, covalent bond between the antibody and aminated DNA barcode,\nL551@P3: while the other method, CITE-seq18 conjugates, on average, two bulky\nL552@P3: streptavidin (~50 kDa each) to each antibody (~150 kDa) before non\u0002covalently binding to biotinylated DNA barcodes. Minimizing steric\nL553@P3: hindrance is important in the scalability of the protein assay and in\nL554@P3: the future extension of this approach to intracellular labeling. Here we\nL555@P3: demonstrate the unprecedented scalability of REAP-seq by conjugating\nL556@P3: up to 82 antibodies to unique DNA barcodes.\nL557@P3: REAP-seq is readily adaptable to other microfluidic or micro-/nano\u0002well platforms1,19, and could also be applied to bulk samples to simul\u0002taneously measure both protein and RNA. Recent work demonstrates\nL558@P3: that scRNA-seq on fixed cells is possible20, and we anticipate REAP\u0002seq being extended to measure intracellular signaling pathways, B- or\nL559@P3: T-cell receptor sequencing, and other genomic DNA readouts such as\nL560@P3: point mutations and copy-number variations. REAP-seq could also be\nL561@P3: coupled with multiple types of perturbations such as small molecules,\nL562@P3: RNA interference, CRISPR, and other gene editing techniques to provide\nL563@P3: mechanistic insights into cellular phenotypes in relation to disease and\nL564@P3: treatment response.\nL565@P3: Methods\nL566@P3: Methods, including statements of data availability and any associated\nL567@P3: accession codes and references, are available in the online version of\nL568@P3: the paper.\nL569@P3: Note: Any Supplementary Information and Source Data files are available in the\nL570@P3: online version of the paper.\nL571@P3: ACKNOWLEDGMENTS\nL572@P3: We acknowledge R. Riener for help with staining and profiling Rhesus blood\nL573@P3: blocked with aCD27 drug.\nL574@P3: AUTHOR CONTRIBUTIONS\nL575@P3: V.M.P. and J.A.K. conceived the method. V.M.P., J.A.K, J.W., T.K.M., and S.S.\nL576@P3: designed experiments. V.M.P, J.W., N.K., D.C.W., L.L., and R.M. conducted\nL577@P3: experiments. K.X.Z. wrote analysis software. K.X.Z., V.M.P, N.K., J.A.K., R.M., and\nL578@P3: S.S. analyzed the data. V.M.P., J.A.K., and K.X.Z. wrote the manuscript with input\nL579@P3: from all authors.\nL580@P3: COMPETING FINANCIAL INTERESTS\nL581@P3: The authors declare no competing financial interests.\nL582@P3: Reprints and permissions information is available online at http://www.nature.com/\nL583@P3: reprints/index.html. Publisher’s note: Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to\nL584@P3: jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations.\nL585@P3: 1. Macosko, E.Z. et al. Cell 161, 1202–1214 (2015).\nL586@P3: 2. Zheng, G.X. et al. Nat. Commun. 8, 14049 (2017).\nL587@P3: 3. Villani, A.-C. et al. Science 356, eaah4573 (2017).\nL588@P3: 4. Rizvi, A.H. et al. Nat. Biotechnol. 35, 551–560 (2017).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComparative studies of 2168 plasma proteins measured by two affinity-based platforms in 4000 Chinese adults | Nature Communications (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-025-56935-2)\nciteturn40view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn36view1\",\"pattern\":\"IL6\"}); Redirected to URL: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-025-56935-2?error=cookies_not_supported&code=14fd028d-8c3a-4830-8909-f1eb55de6e81; Total lines: 762\nNo matching text found for \"IL6\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLarge-scale plasma proteomics comparisons through genetics and disease associations | Nature (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-06563-x)\nciteturn40view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn36view2\",\"pattern\":\"IL-6\"}); Redirected to URL: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-06563-x?error=cookies_not_supported&code=f57098a4-c007-4ecc-a887-ec42c028bb46; Total lines: 991\nL143: The Spearman correlation between levels of NFL on the two platforms was low (r = 0.06) and cis pQTLs for the protein were not detected on either platform. Many proteoforms can be derived from a single gene, for example through differential splicing of transcripts, proteolytic cleavage and post-translational modification^{cite109†18 }. NFL forms polymers^{cite110†19 }, and we do not have information about which proteoforms are measured on the two platforms.\nL144: Consistent with the results for NFL on Olink, high NFL levels in cerebrospinal fluid and blood have been reported to associate with advanced Alzheimer’s disease^{cite111†20 }. Plasma levels of NFL measured using the Olink platform and an alternative affinity-based assay (Simoa) have been reported to be strongly correlated^{cite112†21 ,cite113†22 } (r > 0.90 for both studies), whereas levels of NFL measured using the SomaScan platform did not correlate with Simoa measurements (n = 231 Icelanders, Spearman r = 0.00).\nL145: Of note, NFL levels measured with both the Olink and SomaScan platforms correlate strongly with Alzheimer’s disease, but with opposite directions of effect. Although Olink and Simoa appear to be measuring the same proteoform(s), it remains to be understood what protein or proteoform SomaScan is measuring.\nL146: For IBD (n = 618 cases in Iceland and n = 900 cases in UKB-BI), prostaglandin-H2 d-isomerase (encoded by PTGDS) was the most significantly associated protein with the disease on both Olink and SomaScan (Supplementary Table cite98†18 ).\nL147: On both platforms, IBD cases had higher plasma levels of prostaglandin-H2 d-isomerase than controls (UKB-BI with Olink: OR = 1.67 per s.d., P = 4.7 × 10^{−40}; Iceland with SomaScan: OR = 1.36 per s.d., P = 1.9 × 10^{−13}), consistent with the reported role of the PGD2 metabolic pathway in IBD supported by animal models^{cite114†23 ,cite115†24 }. The correlation of levels between platforms was 0.51 and cis pQTLs were observed for PTGDS on both platforms.\nL148: Whereas the level of several proteins as measured on both platforms are significantly associated with IBD (for example, CXCL11 and REG3A), some associations did not replicate between the two groups (for example, interleukin-6 (IL-6), MMP12 and CLPS) (Supplementary Table cite98†18 ).\nL149: Of note, IBD was associated with CXCL9 levels as measured by the single Olink assay, but only one of two assays on SomaScan (Supplementary Table cite98†18 ), again raising questions about what proteoform or protein is being targeted with the non-associating SomaScan assay.\nL150: ## Detection of pQTLs\nL151: \nL152: We updated our previous pQTL analysis in Iceland to include more sequence variants and applied the manufacturer-recommended data normalization^{cite77†2 } (Table cite116†1 , Extended Data Fig. cite117†4 , Supplementary Tables cite98†19 and cite98†20 and Supplementary Note cite99†4 ). We identified 2,120 and 22,616 sentinel cis and trans pQTLs, respectively (Table cite116†1 ).\nL153: \nL154: Table 1 Summary of pQTLs detected on the Olink Explore 3072 and SomaScan v4 platforms\nL155: Using the Olink-UKB data, we identified pQTLs stratified by ancestry, using the same approach as we previously applied to the Icelandic SomaScan data^{cite77†2 } (P < 1.8 × 10^{−9}; Table cite116†1 , Extended Data Fig. cite117†4 , Supplementary Tables cite98†21 –cite98†23 and Supplementary Note cite99†8 ). We identified 2,102, 900 and 714 sentinel cis pQTLs for the UKB-BI, UKB-AF and UKB-SA ancestry groups, respectively, and 24,824, 1,332 and 190 sentinel trans associations.\nL156: Of the UKB-BI sentinel cis pQTL associations, 1,246 were from the initial set (1536) and 856 were from the Expansion set, whereas of the trans pQTL associations, 16,551 were from the 1536 set and 8,273 were from the Expansion set.\nL157: Based on Olink, 52% of sentinel trans pQTL associations are with a variant associating with more than 10 proteins (non-specific pQTL), whereas based on SomaScan, 63% of trans pQTL associations are with such a variant. Consequently, 211 out of all 2,616 proteins with at least one pQTL on Olink (8%) have associations with only non-specific trans pQTLs and 1,282 out of 4,574 proteins with at least one pQTL on SomaScan (28%) had such associations only.\nL158: When an assay has no cis pQTLs and has only non-specific trans pQTLs, it is possible that the targeting or measurement are not accurate. Non-specific pQTLs should be interpreted with caution.\nL159: In both the UKB-BI Olink data and Icelandic SomaScan data, more than 98% of the cis pQTLs associate with only one protein in cis. However, 34 pQTLs detected in the Olink data had multiple cis associations. Of these, 32 associated with fewer than 25 proteins in cis or trans—mostly 2 or 3—whereas 2 associated with more than 25 (768 and 388). On SomaScan, 34 pQTLs had multiple cis associations.\nL160: Of these, 29 associated with less than 25 proteins in cis or trans, whereas 5 associated with more than 25 (ranging from 40 to 786) (Supplementary Tables cite98†19 –cite98†21 ). Co-regulation of expression in cis has been observed at RNA level and does not in itself detract from the cis pQTL as evidence for the performance of the assay^{cite118†25 }. However, the cis location of a highly pleiotropic variant could be a matter of chance and should therefore be considered in the same way as a trans pQTL.\nL161: The detection of cis pQTLs for a given protein associated positively with the fraction of assay measurements above the LOD (P = 8.3 × 10^{−164}, median fraction above LOD 99.8%) as well as the fraction of assays where the median normalized protein expression (NPX) value was above the LOD (chi-squared P = 2.7 × 10^{−207}), based on 24 healthy and 48 individuals with disease (information supplied by Olink) (Supplementary Fig. cite99†6 and Supplementary Tables cite99†11 and cite98†24 ).\nL162: However, we detected pQTLs for some assays with a very small fraction of values above the LOD, indicating that measurements below the LOD may still be informative.\nL163: Neither subsampling nor accounting for the different number of variants tested in the datasets affected the conclusions of the comparison between the platforms. In both datasets, we estimated a false discovery rate of 1.2% (Supplementary Note cite99†9 ).\nL164: Replication of pQTLs in the Icelandic dataset also assessed by Olink was somewhat lower than predicted by power analysis and may represent the effect of differences in population and sample handling and processing (Supplementary Note cite99†10 and Supplementary Tables cite99†13 and cite98†25 )\n"
